# Chapter 7 · Structural Functionalism and Gender

> Structural functionalism was a 2019 short note, and a 2019 MCQ asked what it is related to (the **stability** of society). It is also part of any "critical gender analysis of development theories" (2024).

## 7.1  What structural functionalism says

::: define Structural functionalism | a theory of social order
The view that society is like a **living body**: a system of interrelated parts (institutions such as the family, education, religion, economy and government), each performing a **function** that keeps the whole **stable** and in balance (**equilibrium**). Social arrangements exist because they are **useful** to society.
-> Key words: system, function, structure, stability, consensus, equilibrium, socialisation, roles.
:::

- **Émile Durkheim** (1858–1917) laid the foundations: society is held together by shared values and a **division of labour**.
- **Talcott Parsons** (1902–1979), the leading American sociologist of the 1950s, built the full theory.
- **Robert Merton** added **manifest** (intended) and **latent** (unintended) functions, and **dysfunctions**.
- **George Murdock** (*Social Structure*, ==1949==) argued that the **nuclear family** is universal and performs four functions: **sexual, reproductive, economic and educational**.

::: simple
A functionalist looks at any custom and asks: "What does this do for society?" If men and women have different roles, the functionalist answer is: "Because the difference helps society run smoothly."
:::

## 7.2  Parsons on gender: instrumental and expressive roles

::: thinker Talcott Parsons and Robert Bales | *Family, Socialization and Interaction Process* (1955)
In the modern family, the **husband-father** plays the **instrumental role** — earning income and linking the family to the wider world — while the **wife-mother** plays the **expressive role** — caring, nurturing and providing emotional support. This division is **functional**: it avoids competition between spouses and gives children stable models. The family's two key functions are the **primary socialisation of children** and the **stabilisation of adult personalities**.
:::

::: fig compare | Parsons's two roles in the family.
## Instrumental role (husband-father)
- Breadwinner in the labour market
- Links the family to society
- Task-oriented and rational
- Authority and discipline
## Expressive role (wife-mother)
- Homemaker and carer
- Emotional centre of the family
- Nurturing and supportive
- Socialises the children
:::

## 7.3  Structural functionalism and development

Functionalism shaped **modernisation theory** (Chapter 4). Parsons's **pattern variables** contrasted "traditional" societies (ascribed status, particular loyalties, emotion) with "modern" ones (achieved status, universal rules, rationality). Development was seen as a smooth shift towards the modern pattern — including the Western nuclear family with a male breadwinner. Development planners of the 1950s–60s therefore:

- treated men as producers and household heads;
- treated women as mothers and homemakers (welfare, nutrition and family-planning projects);
- assumed that household income was shared fairly.

## 7.4  The gender critique of structural functionalism

1. **It justifies inequality.** By calling the gender division of labour "functional", it presents women's subordination as natural and necessary. Functional **for whom**? For men and employers, not necessarily for women.
2. **It ignores power and conflict.** It assumes **consensus**; it has no place for domestic violence, men's control of money, or conflict of interest between spouses. Conflict theorists and feminists put power at the centre.
3. **It is historically narrow.** The breadwinner–homemaker family was typical of **middle-class, white America in the 1950s** — not of most societies, where women have always worked in fields, markets and crafts.
4. **It ignores women's economic work.** Women's productive work — in farming, home-based production, the informal sector — disappears from view.
5. **It resists change.** A theory of stability explains poorly how and why gender roles **change**.
6. **Biological assumptions.** It treats women's caring role as following from childbirth and nursing, rather than from social construction (Topic 1).

**Critics** include **Betty Friedan** (*The Feminine Mystique*, ==1963==), who showed how unhappy the "expressive" housewife often was; **Ann Oakley** (*The Sociology of Housework*, ==1974==), who studied housework as real, often monotonous work; and Marxist feminists, who showed that the "functional" family serves **capitalism** (Chapter 3).

## 7.4a  Functionalism and development planning: an example

In the 1960s and 1970s, agricultural extension services in many countries trained **men** as farmers and offered women only **home-economics** classes in cooking and sewing — because, in functionalist terms, the man was the "instrumental" provider and the woman the "expressive" homemaker. In regions where women did much of the farming, this wasted their knowledge and lowered productivity. Pakistan's own extension services long followed this pattern; women livestock keepers and farmers still receive far less advice and credit than men.

## 7.5  What remains useful

- It reminds us that gender roles are tied to **institutions** — family, school, religion, economy — and that change in one affects the others.
- Its concept of **socialisation** explains how children learn gender roles.
- Its concept of **dysfunction** can be used critically: rigid gender roles are **dysfunctional** when they waste women's talent, harm health or keep families poor.

::: pakistan | Functionalist ideas in Pakistan
The idea that men are **providers** (*kafeel*) and women **homemakers** is strong in Pakistani society and appears in textbooks, sermons and dramas. It is often defended as "functional" for family stability. Yet most rural women already do heavy **productive work** in fields and with livestock, and rising prices push more urban families to need **two incomes**. Male migration leaves many women running households alone. The functionalist picture no longer fits much of Pakistani reality — which is why its gender critique matters for policy.
:::

::: eye | Short note on structural functionalism (2019)
Define it (society as a system of functioning parts; stability — the MCQ key). Durkheim, Parsons, Murdock (1949). Parsons and Bales (1955): instrumental and expressive roles. Link to modernisation and development planning. Four or five critiques (justifies inequality; ignores power; 1950s middle-class model; ignores women's work; resists change). One Pakistani example. A small compare figure of the two roles.
:::
